<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atlantic Ocean</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atlantic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atlantic Ocean separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. From the fifteenth century it became the main highway of European colonial expansion, used by the Portuguese, Spanish, British and French empires. It carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which transformed African states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> empires, and in antiquity it marked the outer limit of Punic voyages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean.jpg" alt="Atlantic Ocean" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantic_Ocean_-_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
